{"title":"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We consider a prism which has the upper and inferior basis the pentagons: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/e/e1eef8dc2aafc63d7f66cdc160bf934aeb307b0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{1}A_{2}A_{3}A_{4}A_{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb4f6429aedb4615c244e2630b29a114d3f81276.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{1}B_{2}B_{3}B_{4}B_{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" />. Each of the sides of the two pentagons and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/7/597a7bd4b30b0dc3fb0d5377d2bd4000f6465fcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{i}B_{j}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc01ce0f103601849e3e08742ca7f5e2326be4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i,j=1, \\dots, 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"17\" /> is colored in red or green. In every triangle which has all sides colored there exists one red side and one green side. Prove that all the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> sides of the two basis are colored in the same color.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us prove first that the edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/4/19405a058354442c5ddb3f7c4664bbb6ea17e78f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2,A_2A_3, \\dots, A_5A_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"18\" /> are of the same color. Assume the contrary, and let w.l.o.g. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/1/ef100258eefc3cb01f88675aaf511c44213fbf8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" /> be red and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/b/28b7e9a45c61fbf6c1955fd5f48e0c305a52c559.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2A_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"15\" /> be green. Three of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/343c2c303ade0fa998d8efae84482ea18d820e82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2B_l, (l = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"18\" />, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/6/9b63d715b10ea17749b051360a4604b9a6fd2d4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2B_i,A_2B_j,A_2B_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"17\" />, have to be of the same color, let it w.l.o.g. be red. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/6/d665bd2cf5e63c96d18abbf50f96d04a5ec1414b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1B_i,A_1B_j,A_1B_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"17\" /> must be green. At least one of the sides of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/2/c92b8661686d7895dd98b63c1a04a6ec8d0facdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_iB_jB_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"17\" />, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/e/c8e038b1058c38a4a71702815da652a7e431b69b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" />, must be an edge of the prism. Then looking at the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/e/e0ef7cb2e4c67d0ef0b980c4c3589406de7f3665.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1B_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab7f4e3cd2a4ef3e2a20cc107e7d17e7bed1ac78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2B_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"63\" height=\"19\" /> we deduce that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/e/c8e038b1058c38a4a71702815da652a7e431b69b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"17\" /> can be neither green nor red, which is a contradiction. Hence all five edges of the pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/e/0be9a882d3e97d492288ec42ff95b7608d132cb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" /> have the same color.\n</p><p>Similarly, all five edges of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/f/e0f3fe346864ca5da6443faff932665a21a7beb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1B_2B_3B_4B_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" /> have the same color. We now show that the two colors are the same. Assume otherwise, i.e., that w.l.o.g. the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> edges are painted red and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> edges green. Let us call segments of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/3/bc3c27faa67216fa47ef554a7f92f76b7008de43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> diagonal (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> may be equal). We now count the diagonal segments by grouping the red segments based on their <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> point, and the green segments based on their <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> point. As above, the assumption that three of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/3/bc3c27faa67216fa47ef554a7f92f76b7008de43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_iB_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> for fixed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" /> are red leads to a contradiction. Hence at most two diagonal segments out of each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> may be red, which counts up to at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> red segments. Similarly, at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> diagonal segments can be green. But then we can paint at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> diagonal segments out of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, which is a contradiction. Hence all edges in the pentagons <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/e/0be9a882d3e97d492288ec42ff95b7608d132cb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"104\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/f/e0f3fe346864ca5da6443faff932665a21a7beb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1B_2B_3B_4B_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" /> have the same color.\n</p><p>This solution was posted and copyrighted by Learner94.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, notice that colorings described in the problem are possible.\nFor example, take all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa8a94d25318af8b16b2441f7f697b0fe2952332.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i B_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"42\" height=\"19\" /> to be red, and all sides of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/f/eaf563e9b672c89895bb31ed75fb723318e91e7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1 A_2 A_3 A_4 A_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/c/eccdfd3cf4977b4666106dc0efecdad9f8b446ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1 B_2 B_3 B_4 B_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"17\" /> to be green. We\ncan just as well take one (or even several) of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa8a94d25318af8b16b2441f7f697b0fe2952332.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i B_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"42\" height=\"19\" /> to be\ngreen as well.\n</p><p>We will now consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197151a067f49bd4daa1952d1a4fac322409f0a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> matrix\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/4373f6d50b08390e57f85e72f80b7c5b9c8e580a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} A_1 B_1 &amp; A_1 B_2 &amp; A_1 B_3 &amp; A_1 B_4 &amp; A_1 B_5 \\\\ A_2 B_1 &amp; A_2 B_2 &amp; A_2 B_3 &amp; A_2 B_4 &amp; A_2 B_5 \\\\ A_3 B_1 &amp; A_3 B_2 &amp; A_3 B_3 &amp; A_3 B_4 &amp; A_3 B_5 \\\\ A_4 B_1 &amp; A_4 B_2 &amp; A_4 B_3 &amp; A_4 B_4 &amp; A_4 B_5 \\\\ A_5 B_1 &amp; A_5 B_2 &amp; A_5 B_3 &amp; A_5 B_4 &amp; A_5 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"291\" height=\"97\" />\n</p><p>This matrix is cyclic in the sense that the 1st and 5th rows are\nconsidered adjacent, and the 1st and 5th column are considered\nadjacent. When we want to express that some of the entries are\nred or green, we will replace those entries by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. (The\nlocation in the matrix will make it clear which entry we are\nreferring to.)\n</p><p>Since every colored triangle has to have both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, notice\nthat if two entries in adjacent rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/ece31187a8c6eba53c1e264cec36fbbd7f76b474.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i, i+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"17\" /> with the same index\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/8122aa89ea6e80784c6513d22787ad86e36ad0cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"10\" height=\"17\" /> are of the same color (say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />) then the segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/d/a2d3668fca6bc2d4393f7e8c6c8101549afd167f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i A_{i+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" />\nis of the opposite color (in this case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />). For example if we have\nthe sub-matrix of rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/c/1ac8effbba9c83e52fc90ea5a0c3269f872dbbbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2, 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/5/175de1e35b533478928277d9fee386b3970dd5f1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} A_2 B_1 &amp; A_2 B_2 &amp; A_2 B_3 &amp; r &amp; A_2 B_5 \\\\ A_3 B_1 &amp; A_3 B_2 &amp; A_3 B_3 &amp; r &amp; A_3 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"254\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/5/cc5777e3a61045b8c8b6d25ffd4642ef8b953d61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_2 A_3 = g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />. Similarly, if we have to sub-matrix of columns\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bfbaae6caa92f2c9f580ab0b49aed29a07323cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5, 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f939c2e6a9eca985800081959f1c56e4d68e0ab.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} A_1 B_1 &amp; A_1 B_5 \\\\ g &amp; g \\\\ A_3 B_1 &amp; A_3 B_5 \\\\ A_4 B_1 &amp; A_4 B_5 \\\\ A_5 B_1 &amp; A_5 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"114\" height=\"97\" />\n</p><p>then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/f/c4fb37e59813c14420df59d48db0b065ab19e3e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1 B_5 = r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"17\" />. Thus, we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/9/389cd708e503af5c672857b4af5b0dbb681d4ed9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Observation}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"112\" height=\"14\" />: We can not have two columns\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce8c1b00f446e3a3c1a6d3114e27f359fe7503e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" /> in two adjacent rows in the\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197151a067f49bd4daa1952d1a4fac322409f0a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> matrix.\n</p><p>Similarly, we can not have two rows\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/9/1495d47611bff986eea3bfe4227ab72d80d81f0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r &amp; r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"46\" height=\"22\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/6/8f6e2fd544a6767f34afe8f83db1bb4299b5e73b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g &amp; g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"47\" height=\"22\" /> in two adjacent columns in\nthe <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/7/197151a067f49bd4daa1952d1a4fac322409f0a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 \\times 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> matrix.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/3/d83ecfbecd575ffd5b4de40f5e95d21869e16644.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Lemma}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"69\" height=\"14\" />: Every pair of adjacent rows has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e03b602154bf17643e7945df4dae9dcd3a22081.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" />\ncolumn with entries of the same color. Also, if the color is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd55b7206a2ad9956bd1118b4351d8827b05b8bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\#(r) &gt; \\#(g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> (and similarly for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />). (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/6/216c8aa49af6a24c26d80e1b55b6ad4c790a9cb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\#(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> represents the number\nof entries of color <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in the row.)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/0/580ed714d1553bf5ba58b71cd51d2d813313aaeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{Proof}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" />: To make writing simpler, we take rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/5/6a5c35cf5914d4f296f3ba9cec0e836ccca18744.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} A_1 B_1 &amp; A_1 B_2 &amp; A_1 B_3 &amp; A_1 B_4 &amp; A_1 B_5 \\\\ A_2 B_1 &amp; A_2 B_2 &amp; A_2 B_3 &amp; A_2 B_4 &amp; A_2 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>If the first column is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" /> or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce8c1b00f446e3a3c1a6d3114e27f359fe7503e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" /> then we are done. If not, let\nus say we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/2/d729bc9f1c1799b641f6b81536a22c5d283ef623.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} r &amp; A_1 B_2 &amp; A_1 B_3 &amp; A_1 B_4 &amp; A_1 B_5 \\\\ g &amp; A_2 B_2 &amp; A_2 B_3 &amp; A_2 B_4 &amp; A_2 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>If the second column is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" /> or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce8c1b00f446e3a3c1a6d3114e27f359fe7503e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" /> then we are done.\nIf not, the second column can not be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/e/48e7b458af132ee634cf9c5d3c8b7d1865cbe020.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" />\nbecause we would have the sub-matrix\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/6/0a6f7439c89f98374dc1ed317f87c92012abcec6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r &amp; r \\\\ g &amp; g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"56\" height=\"45\" />, which is impossible because\nof the second part of Observation 1. So the second column has to be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/9/309e0e3019fddb866a9a6394f6a25b0b1e27fcc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>Continuing this way we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/c/b7c4a32505df79eecbd0c239e230569a1ff7d67e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{bmatrix} r &amp; g &amp; r &amp; g &amp; A_1 B_5 \\\\ g &amp; r &amp; g &amp; r &amp; A_2 B_5 \\end{bmatrix}\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"42\" />\n</p><p>The last column can not be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/9/309e0e3019fddb866a9a6394f6a25b0b1e27fcc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" /> or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/e/48e7b458af132ee634cf9c5d3c8b7d1865cbe020.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" /> because of the second part of\nObservation 1 (recall that columns <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are adjacent!). So\nit has to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" /> or\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce8c1b00f446e3a3c1a6d3114e27f359fe7503e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>Let us say we have a column <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" />.\nTo show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd55b7206a2ad9956bd1118b4351d8827b05b8bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\#(r) &gt; \\#(g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" /> in the first row, we notice that if\nwe have a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in the row, it has to be from a column\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/9/309e0e3019fddb866a9a6394f6a25b0b1e27fcc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" />. But we can not have two\nsuch columns adjacent, so there is room for at most two of them.\nA similar argument applies to the second row, which concludes the\nproof of the Lemma.\n</p><p>Now to solve the given problem, let a matrix of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5f43501ce07aefde69152de3d82dd060366bcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A_i B_j)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"56\" height=\"20\" /> be\ngiven such that it conforms to being colored in such a way that\nevery colored triangle contains both colors. Looking at the\nsub-matrix of row <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and row <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we must have a column with\nthe same color, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" />.\nLooking at rows <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, there is a sub-matrix which has a\ncolumn which is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" />\nor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce8c1b00f446e3a3c1a6d3114e27f359fe7503e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} g \\\\ g \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"30\" height=\"45\" />. It has to be a column\nof <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />'s because row <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> has to have at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a75ba8cf564792f6f276ed68714a1c5dd649caf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\ g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" />'s and at least\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee2e3104a7ae6fc515706729c5879bcad722976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />'s. Continuing this process, we see that every pair of rows\nhas a column <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/c/c2ce6fcecaf35323473927a6338a6d75e60e7329.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{bmatrix} r \\\\ r \\end{bmatrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"29\" height=\"45\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/6/aa627cb837431f451698ec940e275e5a5f95cfce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(A_1 A_2, A_2 A_3, A_3  A_4, A_4 A_5, A_5 A_1) = (g, g, g, g, g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"376\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>By a similar argument applied to columns,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/4/5743d9b91192ec93ad1ddef0605ccc9a793614bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(B_1 B_2, B_2 B_3, B_3  B_4, B_4 B_5, B_5 B_1) = (g, g, g, g, g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"378\" height=\"20\" />\nor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/e/4de0163d82603c13f3965ef38ac4c552c7f99059.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(B_1 B_2, B_2 B_3, B_3  B_4, B_4 B_5, B_5 B_1) = (r, r, r, r, r)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"371\" height=\"20\" />.\nNote that from the lemma it follows that each row has at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a75ba8cf564792f6f276ed68714a1c5dd649caf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\ g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" />'s,\nand at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee2e3104a7ae6fc515706729c5879bcad722976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />'s. So the matrix has at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edcd3ee53661a71dfe323774eedef7006b620fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\ g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"17\" />'s and at least\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/0/3402b077b76237fd34c2bbf8ceb093cafff98ac4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" />'s. It follows that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/a/44a6337fe10e7f8affe3923e8a7cec975ee6f6f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(B_1 B_2, B_2 B_3, B_3  B_4, B_4 B_5, B_5 B_1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"261\" height=\"20\" /> has to be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/d/21d727ace0fb0de51928a22a1c6428cc0dbc5f7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(g, g, g, g, g)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"95\" height=\"20\" />, since if they were <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />'s, the matrix would have\nto have at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/3/ab37cca45daebf1cd853ad70d9c77acb85d5b8a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15\\ g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"17\" />'s and at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/e/28eac82756cb2728ed098a2c2aad81fc694a3a77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10\\ r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"34\" height=\"14\" />'s.\n</p><p>[Solution by pf02, October 2026]\n</p><p><br />\nThe original thread for this problem can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p2448979\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO\" title=\"1979 IMO\">1979 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1979_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1979 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017173104\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.042 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.055 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 459/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 895/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 22687/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.959      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.959      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:20527:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017173104 and revision id 293129. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}